 #uuid "af69ae9b-2ba9-5aaa-acb9-2b7135b354d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570669 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bb3e3723-e2fb-5d7d-804d-fb8906580c7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570678 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d4a445c9-d381-5c2e-b303-52cf74b14cc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570694 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc9a310b-5a8f-5f99-b66d-f83152a549f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570704 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570704 #edb/ref 1069 "q-357229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570704 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570704 #edb/ref 1071 "The temperature of a cold room decreases by $\\frac{3}{8}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\frac{10}{3}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1692e268-919b-5ead-b3e6-7be50cf4ba12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570704 #edb/ref 1074 "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{8}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{10}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{8}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})=-\\frac{10\\times 3}{3\\times 8}=-\\frac{10}{8}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{5}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570711 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570711 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570711 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570711 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $7(x+8)^{3}=448.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2640409b-d45b-5385-980a-142f0264d172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570711 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+8)^{3}$ term and then taking the $3$rd root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+8)^{3}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x+8)^{3} & =448 \\\\ (x+8)^{3} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $3$rd root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+8)^{3} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(x+8)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x+8 & =\\sqrt[3]{{4}^{3}} \\\\ x+8 & =4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570713 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570713 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570713 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570713 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{36{t}^{2}}{25}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570713 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a409742-71a6-54c6-bd19-7151d386a675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570713 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{36{t}^{2}}{25}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{25}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(6t)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|6t|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|6|\\cdot |t|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\cdot |t|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{6|t|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570724 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570724 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570724 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3})\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as an inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570724 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "461aef2d-ef91-5bc9-a05c-59153c1a3f67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570724 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](images/73/73f3c747217e98926510918c395eb644eb143ba07446a863b3f038aafb3fd19a.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3})$ can be expressed as the inequality $x<\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $(3,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x>3.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx<\\sqrt{3}\\text{ or }x>3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570766 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570766 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570766 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f6/f6408a8a100f18484b72e6a21243111c4445240a93f84068486e03290a2f8892.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac232906-fa2f-5902-9534-48663339125f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570766 #edb/ref 1074 "The element shown in the diagram is the part of the circle that is enclosed between the points $B$ and $D$ on the circle and passes through the point $C$ on the circle. So, this element represents the arc $\\overset{⌢}{DCB}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570774 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570774 #edb/ref 1071 "For what value of $k$ are the lines $3x+4y=8$ and $kx+4y=-20$ parallel?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb6eb9c0-dc4b-52b4-82f6-8cb252573f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570774 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $3x+4y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4y & =8 \\\\ 4y & =-3x+8 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx+4y=-20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+4y & =-20 \\\\ 4y & =-kx-20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{4}x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\frac{k}{4}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $-\\frac{k}{4}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{4} & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570777 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570777 #edb/ref 1069 "q-287666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570777 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570777 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(p-1)x=5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570777 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db12ae2f-b0be-5f71-b38b-bd9f3742126a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570777 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2(p-1),\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-1)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-1)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-1 & =0 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570782 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570782 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570782 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570782 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d4c1c22e521c157ee7e8d00e4ca545b98d04f56721c58c0b6ec46c2126e4f2ff.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a regular nonagon and a regular hexagon. Find the measure of the shaded angle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570782 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8619f65-a151-5133-8a41-d7413e4c5c11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570782 #edb/ref 1074 "The shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular nonagon and a regular hexagon.\n\n![](images/a6/a64e0e57966f7b99b6d0f0af843dff25b7a87ed77347643212e04b0f5b8f5cd7.png)\n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum to ${360}^{∘}.$ In a regular nonagon, there are $9$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{9}={40}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a regular hexagon, there are $6$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{6}={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given angle has the following measure:\n\n$$\n{40}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={100}^{∘}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570790 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following tables describes a function that has $-5$ and $5$ as roots?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fadbbd96-8a4c-5964-b393-9b0639ffb5cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570790 #edb/ref 1074 "The numbers $-5$ and $5$ are roots of $g(x)$ if $g(-5)=0$ and $g(5)=0.$  Of the given tables, only the following table has this property:\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $5$ | $2$ | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570800 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570800 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19e9f33b-18e8-5eaa-bf1a-0b161d41f50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570800 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186570724 #